This is a famous open problem in mathematics. The problem statement itself acknowledges that "it is an open problem whether any of the classical constants π, e, √2, ln 2, or φ is normal in any base." The theorem is tagged `@[category research open, AMS 11]`, confirming its status as an open research conjecture.

No proof is currently known. Establishing the normality of π in base 10 (or any base) would require showing that every finite block of digits of length $k$ occurs in the decimal expansion of $\pi$ with asymptotic frequency $10^{-k}$, for every $k$. Despite billions of computed digits showing near-uniform distribution, no rigorous proof exists for any classical irrational constant. This remains one of the major unsolved problems in number theory.

